  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nTHUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nLATE THIS AFTERNOON--EVENING...An isolated thunderstorm or two is \npossible generally along/north of I-70 between about 5-10 PM in the \nvicinity of a stalled weak frontal zone draped across central to \nnorthern KS. Rather weak forcing along with some capping issues \nshould keep activity quite isolated, so most locations should remain \nthunderstorm-free. The only caveat would be if outflow from the \ncurrent eastern NE thunderstorm complex continues moving south, \nsharpening this frontal zone and increase convergence, supporting \nsomewhat better chances for isolated thunderstorms. The main hazard \nwill likely be isolated severe wind gusts given inverted-v \nthermodynamic profile, although modest effective deep layer shear \nmay also support lower-end severe hail with the tallest cores.\n\nSUNDAY LATE AFTERNOON--WEDNESDAY EVENING...Sunday through Wednesday \nlook like carbon copies of each other, with isolated thunderstorm \nchances each late afternoon-evening in vicinity of another stalled \nfrontal zone that'll begin situating itself across Mid-America \nSunday. Weak ripples in the mid/upper flow will also aid in these \nchances. Similar to today, thinking these isolated chances will be \n\"highest\" generally along/north of I-70, although activity could \nreach as far south as roughly Highway 56. Similar to today, rather \nweak forcing and some capping issues should keep most locations \nthunderstorm-free. The main hazard will likely be severe wind gusts \ngiven inverted-v thermodynamic profiles, although modest deep layer \neffective shear may support marginally severe hail as well with the \ntallest cores.\n\nTHURSDAY--NEXT WEEKEND...Per deterministic and ensemble consensus, a \ndeepening upper trough across central to eastern North America \nshould support the stalled frontal zone to sharpen some and finally \nbegin to progress south through the region late week through next \nweekend. This should support increasing chances for off-and-on \nthunderstorms expanding south to include much of the region, mainly \nduring the late afternoon-evening-night-morning hours each day. \nThere remains quite a bit of uncertainty on the speed and placement \nof the frontal zone given significant differences on the \naforementioned upper trough location and magnitude, which makes the \nthunderstorm forecast highly uncertain. Stay tuned as we refine \nforecast details in the coming days.\n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nTHROUGH WEDNESDAY...Persistent central/southern CONUS upper ridging \nin concert with breezy/gusty south winds will support above average \ntemperatures through at least mid-week. Low-level thickness values \nsupport widespread upper 90s and 100s during the day, with overnight \nlows likely no cooler than the upper 70s to low 80s. Forecast highs \nmay actually be a tad conservative (especially along/west of I-135) \ngiven the mostly dry weather and associated lower soil moisture the \npast several weeks. Two saving graces during this upcoming warm \nspell are the likelihood of lower dewpoints compared to the July \nheat waves and stout/gusty south winds, which will aid in \nevaporative cooling effects on skin. Heat indices around 100-106 \ndegrees are likely each day, highest generally over \nsouthern/southeast KS during the afternoon through early evening, so \nlater shifts may need to consider Heat Advisory headlines at some \npoint for portions of the area.\n\nTHURSDAY--NEXT WEEKEND...A stalled frontal zone should eventually \nmove south through the region sometime late week through next \nweekend, supporting somewhat cooler temperatures in its wake. The \ndegree of cooling and when the cooler air arrives is highly \nuncertain and will be tied to frontal zone timing and magnitude. \nCooler scenarios support widespread daytime highs in the 80s \n(especially next weekend), while warmer scenarios only \"cool off\" \nthe region into the 90s. Stay tuned as we refine forecast details in \nthe coming days.",
